    "q-150345": {
      "dom_id": "step-q150345",
      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "George rides his bike $\\frac{9}{10}$ of a mile each day, going from home to school and back. Compute how many miles he rides in $5$ days just by going to school and back. Between what two whole numbers does your answer lie?",
      "worked_solution": "To find out how many kilometers George travels in $5$ days just by going to school and back, we have to multiply $\\frac{9}{10}$ by $5.$\n\nTo multiply a fraction by a whole number, we multiply the fraction's numerator by the whole number and keep the denominator the same:\n\n$$\n5\\times \\frac{9}{10}=\\frac{5\\times 9}{10}=\\frac{45}{10}\n$$\n\nWe then reduce this fraction to its lowest terms:\n\n$$\n\\frac{45}{10}=\\frac{45÷5}{10÷5}=\\frac{9}{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we then convert $\\frac{9}{2}$ to a mixed number:\n\n$$\n9÷2=4\\,\\text{R}\\,1=4\\,\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, in $5$ days, George rides a total of $4\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{mi},$ which lies between $4$ and $5$ miles.",
